issue.body:
Currently it doesn't look like there is an easy way to write

``` java
MyInterface foo = new MyInterface() {

   @Override
   public void bar() {
      // do something awesome
   }
};
```

A possible api would be

``` java
beginAnonymousInnerClass(String kind);

beginAnonymousInnerClass(String fieldType, String fieldName, Set<Modifier> modifiers, String kind);

endAnonymousInnerClass();
```

The extended begin method would result in

``` java
modifiers fieldType fieldName = new kind() {
```

When `MethodSpec.addCode()` is used as opposed to `addStatement()`, it's often possible to end up with methods that have no trailing newline and thus look like:

```java
void foo() {
  doThing();
  doThing2();}
```

This can easily happen when the body of a method is composed as a `List<CodeBlock>` and then passed to `CodeBlock.join("\n", statements)` (and happens within Dagger often. It's coming up now that we've turned off formatting by default).

This is easily avoidable by using `CodeBlock.join("", statements)` and ensuring that each statement uses `CodeBlock.builder().addStatement().build()` instead of `CodeBlock.of()`, or by using `CodeBlock.join("", "\n", "\n", statements) to add a suffix newline. But both of these are awkward and easy to forget.

I think it should be relatively easy, with some possible hackery in `LineWrapper` to retain whether the last character is a `;` and to propagate that information so that `MethodSpec.emit()` can emit an extra newline if necessary.

If you are generating a subclass of a class like this...

``` java
// Parent.java
class Parent {
  static class Optional {}
}

// Generated Child.java
class Child extends Parent {
  java.util.Optional<String> optionalString() {...}
}
```

...then it is not correct to import java.util.Optional and abbreviate to "Optional<String> optionalString()", because the inherited nested Optional takes precedence over the import statement. Since JavaWriter typically doesn't know anything about Parent in this case, there should be some way of telling it to fully-qualify a type even if it would otherwise be inclined to import it.

This is an actual case that occurs with AutoValue in code at Google. (AutoValue doesn't use JavaWriter and can't as long as this bug remains.)
